      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "The time $X,$ in minutes, between two visitors entering a restaurant can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).$ If a visitor has just entered the restaurant, what is the average time until the next visitor enters?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{min}=2\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next visitor is $2$ minutes.",
